.TH CHTTPSVR_SET_ENGINE_NUM_REACTOR_THREADS 3 "2026-07-17" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_set_engine_num_reactor_threads \- set the OS thread count for the shared engine's reactor
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_set_engine_num_reactor_threads(size_t num_threads);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Sets how many OS threads the shared ccol_event_loop reactor devotes to
its own polling and dispatch, following the num_reactor_threads
parameter of ccol_event_loop_create_with_mprocs (see cthreadcomm.h).
A value of 1 means that one thread both polls and dispatches inline;
any larger value means one dedicated polling thread plus
(num_threads - 1) dispatch worker threads.

By default the reactor uses exactly 1 thread, which is what you get
when you never call this function or call it with num_threads == 0.
A larger num_threads starts that many OS threads instead, and a value
of 0 restores the default of 1.

Like chttpsvr_set_engine_mem_mgmt_procs, this function sets a value
that the reactor reads at construction time, so you can call it only
before the first chttpsvr_start call in the process.  You can call it
again after the reactor has stopped completely (that is, after
chttpsvr_engine_wait returns) and before the next chttpsvr_start.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_not_permitted when the reactor is already running; stop it
                  first in that case.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The default is 1, rather than a detected CPU count, because 1 is
faster for the common case:

  Plain HTTP with reused connections, the typical traffic of a
  browser or an API client: one thread gives the best throughput.
  Because header parsing is fast, the dispatch phase of plain HTTP
  has almost no CPU-bound work in it, so more threads only add the
  cost of a hand-off with nothing to run in parallel.

  TLS with reused connections, the typical HTTPS traffic once the
  connection pool of a client is taken into account: this is a real
  trade-off, not a clean win either way.  One thread gives lower
  throughput, but a clearly better and steadier p99 latency than more
  than one dispatch thread.  Most requests on a TLS connection take
  the same cheap steady-state path as plain HTTP; only the handshake
  of the connection pays the expensive part, and that cost is spread
  across every request the connection then serves.

  TLS with no connection reuse at all, where every request pays for a
  new handshake: more than one dispatch thread gives both higher
  throughput and lower p99 latency.  This is a deliberately extreme
  case, not typical traffic.  The asymmetric-crypto cost of a TLS
  handshake is the private-key operation of the server, which is real
  CPU-bound work that benefits from being spread across cores when
  there is enough of it.

A larger num_threads is worth its cost in one case: a steady churn of
connections combined with TLS, where many separate clients each open
a connection for only one request, or a few, before it closes, so a
large part of the total traffic pays the CPU cost of a handshake
instead of spreading it out.  Some deployments really have this
shape, such as a public API that takes many one-off anonymous
clients, a gateway for IoT devices where unreliable networks cause
frequent reconnects, or a webhook receiver that many different
external services call.  Overall, though, it is the less common
shape: most HTTP client software (browsers, most serious HTTP client
libraries, and the chttpclient of this library) pools and reuses
connections precisely to avoid a repeated handshake cost, and a
server behind a load balancer or a reverse proxy often never sees raw
handshake churn from the public internet at all.  A deployment that
knows its own traffic has a lot of churn should raise num_threads;
this function exists for that override.
.fi
